    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p81-q5-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Yes. There are no exceptions under the stated nonzero assumptions.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p81-q5-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 2,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Multiplying by the denominator squared gives two distinct boundary points: ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "x=-\\frac cb",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " is excluded, while ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "x=\\frac{\\frac{a}{d}-c}{b}",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " is included.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $r=-\\dfrac cb$ and $s=\\dfrac{\\frac{a}{d}-c}{b}$. On the original domain $x\\ne r$, $(bx+c)^2>0$. Multiplying by this square gives $$\\begin{align*}a(bx+c)&\\geq d(bx+c)^2\\\\d(bx+c)^2-a(bx+c)&\\leq0\\\\(bx+c)[d(bx+c)-a]&\\leq0\\\\db^2(x-r)(x-s)&\\leq0.\\end{align*}$$ Since $s-r=\\dfrac{a}{bd}\\ne0$, the boundary points are distinct. If $d>0$, the solution lies between them; if $d<0$, it lies outside them. The point $r$ is always excluded because the denominator is zero, while $s$ is included because equality holds there. Thus one boundary is strict and the other includes equality, with no exceptions under the stated assumptions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
